## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement policy-as-code governance for Terraform Cloud, ensuring prod safety beyond backend controls.

## Key Concepts

- Terraform Cloud and Sentinel governance
- Env-to-workspace mapping and state linkage
- CI integration with GitHub Actions for plan/apply gating
- Policy design distinguishing prod vs non-prod changes
- Local and CI testing of policies; post-merge audit of policy outcomes

## Code Example

```sentinel
import "tfplan/v2"

check = func(plan) {
  hasProdChange = false
  for _, rc in tfplan.resource_changes {
    if strings.contains(rc.address, "envs/prod/") {
      hasProdChange = true
      break
    }
  }
  // If prod resources are touched, deny unless approved in PR metadata
  if hasProdChange {
    return false
  }
  return true
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you evolve the policy to support time-bound approvals or multi-degree approvals?
- How would you validate policy effectiveness across 100s of PRs and detect gaps in coverage?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world Terraform governance requires strict isolation and policy enforcement when multiple teams/environments share global resources. This question probes your ability to combine per-environment workspaces with policy guardrails to prevent cross-environment impact.

## Key Concepts
- Per-environment Terraform Cloud workspaces for isolation
- Provider aliases to target the correct AWS account
- Sentinel policy to guard changes to shared VPC components
- CI validation: parse plan JSON and enforce env-tag constraints

## Code Example
```javascript
// Sentinel-like illustrative policy (Terraform Cloud Sentinel syntax would differ)
policy "env_guard" {
  rule deny_shared_vpc_changes {
    // deny any change to shared VPC unless resource.tags.env == workspace.name
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle tag drift across environments?
- How would you test policy changes locally before pushing to CI?
